    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Waterproof was named for its relative safety from flooding before the Mississippi River levee system was built.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterproof%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "First settled around 1830, pioneers chose Waterproof for its fertile soil and proximity to the Mississippi River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/waterproof_louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Great Mississippi River Flood of 1927 inundated over 10,000 square miles across 20 Louisiana parishes, including Tensas, prompting federal levee reinforcement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/waterproof_louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1964, the first 15 black citizens in Waterproof registered to vote after national civil rights legislation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterproof%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Waterproof experiences a humid subtropical climate with hot, humid summers, mild winters, and about 60 inches of annual rainfall.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/waterproof_louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Waterproof lies roughly 10 miles west of the Mississippi River's main channel, near the confluence of the Tensas and Ouachita Rivers.",
